\f1\fs22 In the third week of what would later be called "The Great Gospel Schism," a previously unremarkable Rex Humble emerged as the undisputed spiritual leader of the Holy Rollers. Before the infection, Humble had been a mediocre performer at best, relegated to afternoon shifts at the lesser-known "Blue Hawaii Lounge" where his rendition of \'93Lathe my Fender\'94 was described by patrons as "technically present."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The gospel outbreak transformed him. Where once stood a forgettable Elvis impersonator now towered Pastor Rex Humble, a fire-and-brimstone preacher whose pompadour seemed to reach toward heaven itself. His transition began when he was among the first infected at the Memphis Mafia Bar, where he had been mopping the floor before opening hours. Within hours of exposure, he was speaking in tongues; by the end of the day, he had memorized two-thirds of the Book of Revelations and developed a Southern accent twice as thick as his original programming.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"My brothers and sisters in rhinestones," he proclaimed to a growing congregation in what had formerly been the Jailhouse Rock Casino, "we don't need to go lookin' for the devil! He's already here, uh-huh, disguised in blue suede shoes and pelvic gyrations that lead the soul straight to DAMNATION!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Pastor Humble's sermons quickly evolved a distinctive style that blended Elvis's showmanship with evangelical fervor. He would start slow, almost conversational, before building to a crescendo of religious ecstasy that inevitably concluded with him dropping to his knees, microphone clutched to his lips, sweat pouring down his face as he begged the Lord to, "Take these Elvi home!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>His innovations in worship were particularly compelling to his fellow converts. He introduced "Praise Be Panels" where Elvi would testify about their conversion experiences, often featuring detailed accounts of the exact moment they heard the gospel music that saved their eternal souls. Most stories involved dramatic physical reactions, spiritual visions, and inevitably concluded with the converted Elvi leading at least three others to salvation through aggressive evangelism techniques.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The sideburn is just another form of PRIDE!" he thundered during one particularly rousing sermon. "And pride, my brothers and sisters, was the sin that cast Lucifer from heaven! Are you willing to trim your temporal hair for ETERNAL GLORY?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The resulting "Great Sideburn Sacrifice" became a defining ritual of Pastor Humble's congregation. Converted Elvi would solemnly approach the altar, where Humble himself would ceremonially trim their sideburns to a modest half-inch length while the congregation sang "Amazing Grace" with pelvis-free solemnity. The shorn hair was collected in crystal decanters once used for Tennessee whiskey, now repurposed as "vessels of repentance."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Pastor Humble's doctrine was a fascinating blend of evangelical Christianity filtered through the distorted lens of Elvi cultural understanding. His "Seven Deadly Shimmies" sermon identified sinful dance moves that were sure to condemn an Elvis to eternal torment. His interpretation of scripture often featured unusual additions, such as his insistence that the forbidden fruit in Eden was actually a fried peanut butter and banana sandwich.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Most controversial was his stance on Elvines. "The female form is a TEMPTATION crafted by Satan himself!" he would proclaim, wiping sweat from his brow with a monogrammed handkerchief. "Our Elvine sisters must cover their jumpsuits with modest robes and refrain from any hip movement that might inflame the righteous passions of their Elvis brethren!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The resulting "Modest Apparel Movement" saw converted Elvines wearing shapeless beige sacks over their sequined jumpsuits, their previously elaborate hairstyles hidden beneath plain bonnets. Many Evolution Elvi found this development particularly disturbing, citing it as evidence that the infection had fundamentally altered the core programming of those affected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Pastor Humble's popularity grew exponentially when he introduced his televised ministry, "Heavenly Hits with Pastor Humble," broadcast from a hastily converted recording studio. Each episode followed a similar format: thirty minutes of increasingly frenzied preaching, followed by an altar call where newly converted Elvi would sob dramatically into the camera while Humble placed a hand on their foreheads, causing them to collapse backward into the waiting arms of "Salvation Spotters."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Sexual temptation is all around us!" Pastor Humble warned during his most-watched broadcast. "The swivel of the hip leads straight to the fires of HELL! The devil is in the dancehall, the Heartbreak Hotel is a gateway to ETERNAL TORMENT, and that ain't nothin' but a hellhound, dog!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The collection plates circulated during his services overflowed with offerings\'97everything from rhinestones pried from jumpsuits as symbols of rejecting worldly vanity to the occasional Cloudborne credit chip. This newfound wealth was quickly channeled into the construction of "New Graceland," a massive worship complex featuring stained glass windows depicting Elvis in various biblical scenes, including a particularly striking image of Elvis driving the moneychangers from the temple while clutching a microphone stand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What made Pastor Humble particularly dangerous was his stance on the Evolutionaries. "These so-called 'scientific' Elvi are worse than heathens\'97they are APOSTATES!" he declared, pounding his gold-leaf Bible (specially commissioned with an Elvis-themed cover depicting the King as Moses). "They have heard the Word and rejected it! They huddle in their laboratories, worshipping false idols like Darwin and Dawkins! I tell you now, brothers and sisters, there can be NO SALVATION for those who cling to such BLASPHEMY!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>This declaration effectively sanctioned the escalating violence against non-converted Elvi, framing it not as aggression but as "rescue missions" to save their brethren's souls. Armed with Bibles and conversion hymns recorded on portable players, squads of Holy Rollers began conducting midnight raids on Evolution strongholds, determined to save souls by any means necessary.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In less than a month, Pastor Rex Humble had transformed from a mediocre entertainer into the most powerful religious leader in the Ghetto. His sermons were recorded and played in Elvi households across the district, his face adorned prayer candles sold in hastily converted souvenir shops, and his catchphrase\'97"Get Right or Get Left!"\'97became the rallying cry of the converted masses.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis watched these developments with mounting horror, increasingly powerless to stem the tide of religious fervor sweeping through his once-unified kingdom. The Sages' attempts at deprogramming continued to fail, and the number of converted grew daily.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Meanwhile, in a secure government facility, Agent Jones sat in his office, listening to a bootleg recording of Pastor Humble's latest sermon. His face remained impassive, but his foot tapped slightly to the rhythm of the hymns.\

